{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    /* `ES2022.Error` is added to an otherwise ES2020 program, and only that one sliver of
       ES2022: it declares `ErrorOptions` and the `cause` property, which is what makes
       `throw new Error(msg, { cause })` compile. ESLint's `preserve-caught-error` *requires*
       that form when a catch block rethrows with a new message, so without this line the lint
       rule and the type checker contradict each other and a catch block cannot be written at
       all — which is exactly what happened in `useDeckMeta`'s tag-read refusal.

       Safe at runtime rather than merely allowed: `Error.cause` is Chromium 93+, and this app
       is a WebView2 webview while the suite runs on Node. Raising `target` or the whole `lib`
       to ES2022 would also be safe, and is deliberately not done here — this is the smallest
       change that resolves the contradiction, and it keeps the emit target where it was. */
    "lib": ["ES2020", "ES2022.Error", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* Bundler mode */
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    /* Linting */
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    /* Path aliases (no baseUrl: deprecated in TS 6, paths resolve from this file) */
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./packages/ui/*"]
    },

    /* This app is a webview: no `process`, no `Buffer`, and `setTimeout` returns a `number`
       and not a `NodeJS.Timeout`. Nothing in `packages/ui/` may be allowed to think otherwise.

       Empty rather than a list, and that is measured: the app relies on no ambient `@types`
       package at all. `vite/client` — which types `import.meta.env` and the `*.css`
       side-effect imports — arrives through the `/// <reference types="vite/client" />` in
       `packages/ui/vite-env.d.ts`, and a triple-slash reference is not gated by this option.

       Know what this does and does not buy, because the difference cost a review: it blocks
       the *automatic* inclusion of `node_modules/@types/*`, and nothing more. A transitive
       `/// <reference types="node" />` inside a dependency's own declarations walks straight
       past it — measured 2026-08-09, with `@types/node` merely present in the tree both
       `vitest` and `vite` pulled it into this program despite this line. **The fence that
       actually holds is that `@types/node` is not installed**; see `.storybook/node-url.d.ts`
       for the shim that keeps it that way. This line is the second lock, not the first. */
    "types": []
  },
  // The shared UI and the three apps' pages: `.storybook` is type-checked by its own
  // `.storybook/tsconfig.json`, because it is the half that needs Node types and this half must
  // never see them (see `types` above). `npm run build` runs both.
  //
  // `packages/ui` and `apps/desktop/src` were one folder, `src`, until 2026-10-08: the shared
  // React frontend, and the desktop app's own entry and boot components over it. Of
  // `apps/desktop` that one folder and no more, because the rest of it is the Rust host and a
  // Vite config.
  //
  // `apps/share/` is the public web viewer — a second React entry point over the same
  // `packages/ui/` components, built by `apps/share/vite.config.ts`. It belongs in **this**
  // program rather than one of its own: it is a webview program with no Node in it, it resolves
  // `@/*` through the same paths, and `npm run share:build` runs plain `tsc` in front of the
  // build. Emphatically not `apps/desktop/tsconfig.node.json`, which is `composite` — a file
  // listed in a referenced project *and* included here demands a built declaration beside it
  // (TS6305), and nothing builds that project.
  //
  // `apps/light/` is the light app — a third React entry over the same `packages/ui/`
  // components, built by `apps/light/vite.config.ts`. In this program for `apps/share/`'s reason:
  // a webview program with no Node in it, resolving `@/*` through the same paths.
  //
  // **Three files under `packages/ui/` are not in this program**: the web host's database Worker
  // and its card scanner's, which run in dedicated Workers and are checked by
  // `packages/ui/tsconfig.web-worker.json`, and its service worker, checked by
  // `packages/ui/tsconfig.web-sw.json` — each under the `WebWorker` lib. That lib and this
  // program's `DOM` declare the same globals differently, so each file is left out here rather
  // than given both. Nothing imports either — the page names them by URL — so the exclusion
  // holds; a module that did import one would pull it straight back in.
  "include": ["packages/ui", "apps/desktop/src", "apps/share", "apps/light"],
  "exclude": [
    ".claude",
    // Each app's Vite config, and the Node file beside one (`apps/light/vite.sw.ts`), is a Node
    // program and this one has no Node types; `vite.*.ts` takes the next one too. They were at
    // the root, outside `include`, until the configs moved into the apps. `dist*` is each app's
    // build output (`dist-mobile`, `dist-web`, `dist-share`), which now sits inside a folder
    // this program includes and holds no TypeScript today only by luck.
    "apps/*/vite.*.ts",
    "apps/*/dist*",
    "apps/light/src-tauri",
    "packages/ui/lib/core/web/worker.ts",
    "packages/ui/lib/core/web/scanWorker.ts",
    "packages/ui/lib/core/web/sw/sw.ts"
  ],
  "references": [{ "path": "./apps/desktop/tsconfig.node.json" }]
}
